## Founding & History
Pressing Matters Cellar Door was founded in 2002 by Greg Melick AO SC and his wife Michelle. Their inspiration came from the potential of the neighboring Morningside Vineyard to produce noble European grape varieties. The first vintage from Pressing Matters was released in 2006.

In 2019, the Hall family joined the Melicks as partners, leading to the acquisition of the Morningside vineyard and significant investment in a new winery and tasting room. Greg Melick retired from the vineyard in 2025, selling his final share of the business to the Fogarty-Hall partnership. The Fogarty family joined the Hall's in 2025 to form this new partnership.

The name "Pressing Matters" was inspired by a print by Tasmanian artist Tom Samek, who also designed the original label. The original label featured grapes being squashed inside a law book, a reference to the founders' legal background.

## People
The current ownership of Pressing Matters is a partnership between the Hall family and the Fogarty family, forming Fogarty-Hall, as of 2025. Peter Fogarty and his family joined the Hall's to drive future growth.

The principal winemaker is Liam McElhinney, who took over in 2025 after Sam Connew departed to focus on her Stargazer wines. McElhinney is also responsible for Ossa and Lowestoft wines, other Tasmanian vineyard interests of Fogarty-Hall. Winemaking for Pressing Matters will continue to be done onsite at the winery, which was built three years prior to January 2026.

Mark Hoey and his team at Peak Viticulture manage the vineyard, focusing on soil health, vine balance, and detailed canopy management. They are involved in establishing new blocks with clonal diversity and tending to the oldest Pinot and Cabernet vines in the district.

## Vineyard & Site
The Pressing Matters vineyard is located in Tea Tree, within Tasmania's Coal River Valley. The vineyard spans 20.6 hectares. Another source states the vineyard area as 22 hectares. The original vineyard site, acquired in 2002, consisted of approximately 14 hectares of grazing land on well-drained, north-east facing slopes with cracking clay over a calciferous base, and was frost-free. The vineyard soil is described as dark with deep limestone layers below, contributing elegance and finesse to the wines. The volcanic limestone and black clay soils are noted for adding minerality and texture to the wines.

Vines were planted from 1980 to 2024. Greg Melick initiated French-style close planting techniques in 1980, with two-meter rows and one-meter vines, a method that maximizes potential from the volcanic limestone. The vineyard features over 30 combinations of clones and rootstocks across various aspects and elevations, creating differences in flavor, texture, and mineral quality.

Varietals planted include Pinot Noir, Riesling, Chardonnay, Cabernet Sauvignon, Shiraz, and Gamay. The original plantings in 2002 included Riesling and Pinot Noir. The vineyard initially had 2.9 hectares of Riesling and 3.8 hectares of Pinot Noir. Pinot Noir and Riesling remain at the core of the range. The orientation of rows varies by variety: north-south for Pinot and east-west for Riesling. Vines are trained to vertical shoot positioning trellis and cane-pruned with high levels of canopy management, including shoot thinning, leaf plucking, and crop thinning.

Pressing Matters adopts a "fairly gentle and organic approach" to disease management, using organic fungicides, with chemicals only used when necessary in tough seasons. Weed control is done by hand or machine, avoiding herbicides. Low-level drip irrigation is used when needed, drawing water from dams. Soil health is maintained through light tillage undervine and in the midrow to bring in oxygen, and green manure crops (legumes and grasses) are used to build nutrients and resilience. Harvest is a mix of hand-picking for delicate Riesling and Pinot, and machine-harvesting for thicker-skinned Cabernet.

## Cellar Door Experience
The Pressing Matters Cellar Door is open daily from 10 am to 5 pm. Bookings are encouraged, but walk-ins are welcome where possible. The tasting room is located in the heart of the vineyard in Tea Tree, offering views down the valley. It is described as intimate and educational.

Visitors can enjoy wine tastings and a wine-by-the-glass list with a small plate menu. The cellar door, completed in 2023, is designed for comfort and elegance. It has an accessible car park and a bathroom with wheelchair access. The winery also provides an EV charger for electric vehicles.

## Food & Hospitality
Pressing Matters offers a small plate menu to accompany wine tastings. No reliable information found from public sources regarding a full restaurant or specific notable dishes beyond small plates.

## Awards & Recognition
Pressing Matters has received numerous awards and recognition for its wines. The winery was awarded the Richard Langdon Trophy for Vineyard of the Year 2024 by the Royal Agricultural Society of Tasmania as part of the Royal Hobart Wine Show.

Their Rieslings have a strong track record, with all but one winning at least a Silver medal, including 6 Trophies and 10 Gold medals. The Pressing Matters R139 Riesling 2022 was awarded Sweet Wine of the Year in the 2025 Halliday Wine Companion Awards, scoring 97 points. The 2022 R0 Riesling scored 93/100 from Halliday Wine Companion.

Pressing Matters Pinot Noir 2023 received 96/100 from Halliday Wine Companion. The 2022 Pinot Noir Cuvée C received a Gold Ribbon from The Real Review, scoring 95/100. Their Pinot Noir is described as "highly desirable" for its concentration and elegance. The winery has accumulated 42 Gold Ribbons, 40 Silver Ribbons, and 14 Bronze Ribbons from The Real Review.

Other specific awards include:
* 2024 Chardonnay: Gold - International Wine Challenge 2026.
* 2024 R0 Riesling: Gold - International Wine Challenge 2026.
* 2024 R69 Riesling: Gold - International Wine Challenge 2026.
* 2024 R9 Riesling: Gold - International Wine Challenge 2026.
* 2024 Rosé: Gold Ribbon - The Real Review.
* Royal Hobart International Wine Show: Trophy for the Most Successful Tasmanian Exhibitor 2009.
* Tasmanian Wine Show: Trophy for the Most Successful Exhibitor - 2010, Best Wine of Vintage for 2008 and 2009.

## Sustainability & Certifications
Pressing Matters adopts a "fairly gentle and organic approach" to disease management in the vineyard, using organic fungicides and minimizing chemical use. Weed control is mechanical or manual, avoiding herbicides. They use green manure crops and light tillage to improve soil health and nutrient recycling.

Beyond the vineyard, the winery has solar panels on its roofs to reduce its carbon footprint. The new winery allows for on-site fruit processing, which reduces CO2 emissions from chilling and transporting fruit. They also provide an EV charger for visitors with electric vehicles. Pressing Matters holds Sustainable Tourism Accreditation by ATIC.

## Visiting Practical Info
Address: 711 Middle Tea Tree Road, Tea Tree, Tasmania, 7017, Australia.
Phone: 0474 380 109.
Website: http://www.pressingmatters.com.au/.
Email: cellardoor@pressingmatters.com.au, mail@pressingmatters.com.au.
Social Media: Facebook, Instagram, Twitter.

The cellar door is a short drive from historic Richmond and approximately a 30-minute scenic drive from Hobart. It is open daily from 10 am to 5 pm, closed on Christmas Day and Good Friday. Reservations are encouraged but walk-ins are welcome. The cellar door is wheelchair accessible. Pressing Matters participates in the Tasmanian Mixed Dozen freight scheme, allowing visitors to ship purchased wines home.

## Notable / Distinctive
Pressing Matters is noted for its early adoption of French-style close planting techniques in Tasmania, starting in 1980, which was rare for the region at the time. This approach prioritizes quality over yield, aiming for "high-density, moderate-yield, high-quality" wines.

The vineyard's unique terroir, characterized by volcanic limestone and black clay soils, contributes distinctive minerality and texture to its wines. The cool climate and valley influences create ideal growing conditions for cool-climate varieties, producing wines with deep fruit, acidity, and structure. Their Pinot Noir exhibits a flinty depth, distinguishing it from other Tasmanian regions. The volcanic soils impart a steely spine to their Rieslings.

The winery has made significant investments, including a new winery completed three years prior to January 2026, and a brand-new cellar door completed in 2023, which allows for on-site bottling and direct-to-customer sales. They also have an informal pact with neighbors to protect the she-oaks of Hammonds Tier, which host native wildlife.

